## Third-party components pulled at build time

The component pulls Espressif sources through the ESP-IDF component manager at
build time (`espressif/esp_video`, `esp_cam_sensor`, `esp_ipa`, `esp_sccb_intf`,
`esp_h264`, optionally `usb_host_uvc`). None of them are vendored here; they
carry their own licenses and are fetched from https://components.espressif.com/.
As shipped in the versions this component pins (checked 2026-07): `esp_video`
and `esp_ipa` are under the ESPRESSIF MIT License; `esp_cam_sensor`,
`esp_sccb_intf`, `esp_h264` and `usb_host_uvc` are under Apache-2.0.

`components/esp_video_camera/cfg/sc202cs.json` is an IPA (image tuning)
configuration file taken from the original pull request.

## In Tessera's screen_camera plugin

This folder is the component as published at github.com/n-IA-hane/esphome-esp-video-camera, release v2026.9.2
(commit 6d247d7), copied without changes. The plugin's own code is in `components/screen_camera/`.

## Changed in Tessera's copy

`__init__.py` takes `jpeg_quality` as esp_video does (1 to 100, higher is better, default 80) instead of 1 to 63
(default 10), and `esp_video_camera.h`'s default follows: the value goes straight to
V4L2_CID_JPEG_COMPRESSION_QUALITY (2026-10-19).

## Changed in Tessera's copy: the sensors

`__init__.py` no longer turns on esp_cam_sensor's sensors (SC202CS, OV5647, SC2336, each with its auto-detect) nor the
SC202CS's colour tuning from `cfg/sc202cs.json`: Screen camera's `components/screen_camera/sensors.py` does, from a
table, with the same Kconfig options and the same build file (2026-10-24). The place is marked "Tessera".

## Changed in Tessera's copy: the sensor's exposure

`esp_video_camera.h` and `.cpp` add `read_sensor_exposure()`, which a raw frame consumer calls on the capture task
to read the sensor's exposure time and gain through esp_video's V4L2 controls (for Screen camera's light level,
2026-10-19). Both places are marked "Tessera".
